From 2db650ce1e51fbd378f29406ffb80eb7de63134a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 09:16:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] perf(desktop): one batched primary read for a
 concrete local profile's sidebar

With registry gateways or remote profiles configured, the Electron intercept
of GET /api/profiles/sessions/sidebar always fanned out to three
/api/profiles/sessions reads. For a concrete profile with no remote override
each of those is a plain primary read, so every sidebar refresh cost three
list requests on the primary gateway (measured 0.75/s, the largest share of
its request time) where its batched /sidebar route answers all three slices
in one read (~5 ms vs ~9.5 ms handler CPU unchanged, 81 vs 112 ms after a
state.db change, 2.2 ms inside its response cache).

After the existing remote/registry and pinned-source guards, such a profile
now reads the primary's /api/profiles/sessions/sidebar once with the original
query, stamps the primary owner tag on unowned rows of all three slices (copy,
no mutation) and returns the batched response unchanged otherwise. Any failure
of that read (older primary 404, timeout, transport) runs today's three-slice
path for that request; nothing is remembered. 'all' and remote-override
profiles keep the three-slice path.

The decision, the stamping and the slice fan-out move to
profile-session-routing.ts (main.ts is at its line cap and shrinks by 3).
